course: dictionary
lesson: 12226
title: Linking ideas with consequently
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Consequently» is a linking word. It tells you that something happened as a result of something else. The first part is the cause, the reason, and the part after «consequently» is the result. «The shop ran out of bread; consequently, Mum bought rolls instead.» Running out of bread is the cause, and buying rolls is the result.
explain: To check the meaning, swap in «as a result» or «because of that». If it still makes sense, «consequently» is right. «The lights went out, and consequently we played board games by candlelight.» Try it: «The lights went out, and as a result we played board games by candlelight.» It still makes sense.
explain: Writers use «consequently» in reports, stories and science work because it sounds more formal than «so». When it starts a new sentence, put a comma after it. When it joins two full sentences, put a semicolon before it.
fact: «Consequently» is an adverb meaning as a result, or because of that.
fact: It links a cause with its result.
fact: Other words that do a similar job are «so», «therefore» and «as a result».
fact: Use «; consequently,» between two full sentences: «It snowed; consequently, the school closed.»
fact: «Consequently» is formal, so you will often see it in exams, reports and science writing.
exercise: «The road was closed; consequently, the bus took a different route.» What does consequently show? | The road was closed for a party | *The bus changed route because the road was closed | The bus was painted red | The bus was late because it was Tuesday
exercise: Which words could replace «consequently» in «It rained all day, consequently the match was cancelled»? | Yesterday | Never | Happily | *As a result
exercise: Choose the best word for the gap: «The bell rang; ____, we lined up at the door.» | *Consequently | Quietly | Under | Yesterday
test: «The milk spilled across the floor; consequently, Sam fetched a mop.» Why did Sam fetch a mop? | Because he wanted a snack | Because the floor was clean | Because it was his birthday | *Because the milk had spilled
test: Which sentence uses consequently correctly? | Consequently is my favourite colour. | We ran home consequently the sun. | *The shop was shut; consequently, we went home. | Consequently, the dog was blue and happy.
exam: Choose the word that shows a result: «The power cut lasted all night; ____, the fridge was warm by morning.» | Sideways | Purple | Hungrily | *Consequently
